Package: os7-automation
Version: @OS7_VERSION@
Architecture: @OS7_ARCH@
Maintainer: Bastian Wirth <bastian.wirth@upinblue.com>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: polkitd, os7-module (= @OS7_VERSION@)
Homepage: https://github.com/upinblue/os7
Description: OS/7 automation host — the fence a job runs inside
 docs/AUTOMATION-PLAN.md phase 1. The slice, the templated job unit and the
 polkit rule that governs starting one — the parts of the job contract that
 must live in a file rather than be assembled at run time.
 .
 THE UNIT IS THE FENCE AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A PACKAGE. `os7-job@.service`
 carries the slice, ProtectSystem=strict, ProtectHome, PrivateTmp,
 NoNewPrivileges, the private /dev and the input channel — the same for every
 job on every machine. Because it ships here it is signed by the repository that
 delivered it, it can be read by an auditor, `grep` can check it, and it rolls
 back with the release. Start-OS7Job adds only the per-run parts — the timeout,
 the limits, the credential, the identity — as a drop-in under /run.
 .
 The alternative was `systemd-run`, which assembles the whole unit out of
 arguments: the fence would then be whatever the caller passed, would exist in
 no file, and would not survive a reboot.
 .
 It implements no policy. What should run, and why, belongs to a product above
 (AU1); this package decides only what a job may reach while it runs.
 .
 It does NOT create the dataset the contract's paths live on.
 `New-OS7ServiceDataset -Name os7-automation` does, and it is a separate verb on
 purpose: a package postinst that created a ZFS dataset would do it in a chroot
 where the pool is not imported, and would report success.
